// A read through an accessor declared `&var self` is refused on a `let` root,
// even though the borrow only READS (SL:borrowing §3).
//
// The root charge follows the declaration, never the body: an accessor that
// takes `&var self` holds its root exclusively for every borrow, a bare
// `borrow` read included, exactly as a `&var self` method call does. A
// read-only RESULT never implies a shared RECEIVER. What lets a read share the
// root is a `&self` accessor, written or derived with `@synthesize(shared)`
// (§4). This one has no twin, and could not derive one: its epilogue writes
// `self`, so `@synthesize(shared)` would be refused.
//
// So the refusal is "this accessor needs a writable root", which is about the
// declaration, not "you may not write this", which would be about the lend.
// Nothing is inferred from the body (§1).
// EXPECT: error
// On a `var` root the same read compiles: an exclusive root charge is legal
// there, and the epilogue counts the visit.

struct Bag { cells: [Int; 4], visits: Int }

extension Bag {
    public func tally(&var self, i: Int) borrows -> &var Int {
        if i < 0 || i >= 4 { panic("Bag.tally: index out of range") }
        lend self.cells[i]
        self.visits = self.visits + 1
    }
}

func main() {
    let frozen = Bag(cells: [10, 20, 30, 40], visits: 0)
    print("{borrow frozen.tally(0)}")
}
